WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.759
In this section, we'll recap the logic created in the previous 205 class.

2
00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.779
We'll examine the shortcomings of the previous system and adapt it to the new system designed

3
00:00:10.779 --> 00:00:14.199
for increased modular functionality.

4
00:00:14.199 --> 00:00:19.160
The previous system was built using a combination of triggers, level actors, and logic nested

5
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.039
within the level blueprint.

6
00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:25.519
The process for activating the sequence would be adding a trigger box and a sequence actor

7
00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:30.920
into the level, script the association between the actors via the level blueprint,

8
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.350
and finally trigger the sequence associated with that specific trigger.

9
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:39.189
While the system worked, the inherent downsides of this implementation were

10
00:00:39.189 --> 00:00:40.189
1.

11
00:00:40.189 --> 00:00:44.570
If we had multiple trigger box actors in our level, specifically targeting each trigger

12
00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.479
box from the level actors would be very cumbersome and easily broken.

13
00:00:49.479 --> 00:00:50.479
2.

14
00:00:50.479 --> 00:00:54.439
The level blueprint contains the code needed to start the sequence.

15
00:00:54.439 --> 00:00:59.579
This might not be an issue if we only use one level, however if we use more than one

16
00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.780
level, we will need to copy and paste the code into each level's level blueprint.

17
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.870
This could lead to inconsistencies and functionality issues between levels.

18
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:15.390
And lastly, if we target our level actors rather than nesting our code within a standalone

19
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:19.310
blueprint, we will need to duplicate the level code for every actor that we want

20
00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.400
to use for sequence activation.

21
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.159
The previous method of constructing code and logic for a sequence activation is perfectly

22
00:01:27.159 --> 00:01:28.159
acceptable.

23
00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:33.560
However, since this code and these systems will likely be used multiple times to trigger

24
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:40.359
many sequences, it would be far more efficient to migrate this code into a reusable standalone

25
00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:41.359
actor.

26
00:01:41.359 --> 00:01:43.680
We will do exactly that in the upcoming sections.
